1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,669
A cheque the bank returns, a bounced

2
00:00:02,669 --> 00:00:04,957
cheque, can end in three ways.

3
00:00:04,957 --> 00:00:08,007
This tutorial follows each one through the
books,

4
00:00:08,007 --> 00:00:10,677
on cases from Palm Ridge's past year.

5
00:00:10,677 --> 00:00:13,727
As the Accountant, open Cheque and Cash
Collection

6
00:00:13,727 --> 00:00:16,396
and search the register for Layla Nasser.

7
00:00:16,396 --> 00:00:20,972
Her cheque 220102 for 15,000 came back
unpaid in February and now

8
00:00:20,972 --> 00:00:25,548
shows Replaced: she gave two cheques of
7,500, and both have cleared.

9
00:00:25,548 --> 00:00:28,979
The 500 cash row is the cheque return
penalty.

10
00:00:28,979 --> 00:00:32,793
Open her Tenant Ledger to see it in the
books.

11
00:00:32,793 --> 00:00:37,368
The return journal debited Rent Receivable
15,000 and credited PDC Receivable: the

12
00:00:37,368 --> 00:00:41,563
cheque no longer covered the debt, so
Layla owed it again.

13
00:00:41,563 --> 00:00:44,232
A 500 penalty was added and paid.

14
00:00:44,232 --> 00:00:48,045
Each replacement wrote its own post-dated
cheque journal, crediting Rent

15
00:00:48,045 --> 00:00:51,858
Receivable with 7,500, so the balance came
back to zero.

16
00:00:51,858 --> 00:00:54,908
Had the replacements come to less, the
shortfall

17
00:00:54,908 --> 00:00:57,959
would have stayed here as money still
owed.

18
00:00:57,959 --> 00:01:02,535
Arjun Mehta settled his returned cheque
330103 in cash at the office.

19
00:01:02,535 --> 00:01:06,348
In the register that is a replacement row
with the

20
00:01:06,348 --> 00:01:10,161
mode Cash, received on the spot: it
debited the Cash

21
00:01:10,161 --> 00:01:13,593
Account and credited PDC Receivable with
14,000, and a

22
00:01:13,593 --> 00:01:17,024
journal voucher later moved the cash to
the bank.

23
00:01:17,024 --> 00:01:18,931
Some debts are never paid.

24
00:01:18,931 --> 00:01:21,981
Daniel Brooks left after his December
cheque bounced.

25
00:01:21,981 --> 00:01:24,650
His contract was terminated and settled
against

26
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:27,320
his deposit, and 11,326.03 was still
unpaid.

27
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:31,514
On his contract, More actions, Write off
shows the bad debt,

28
00:01:31,514 --> 00:01:35,708
written off with a reason and approved by
the Company Admin.

29
00:01:35,708 --> 00:01:40,284
Approval posted one journal, debiting Bad
debts written off and crediting Rent

30
00:01:40,284 --> 00:01:44,479
Receivable, and took the debt off every
list of money due.

31
00:01:44,479 --> 00:01:47,148
In July a collection agency recovered
5,000.

32
00:01:47,148 --> 00:01:50,198
The recovery journal debited the bank and
credited

33
00:01:50,198 --> 00:01:53,249
Bad debts recovered, which counts as other
income.

34
00:01:53,249 --> 00:01:56,299
The write-off stays, so the history is
honest.

35
00:01:56,299 --> 00:01:59,350
Back on Cheque and Cash Collection,
Daniel's cheque

36
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:02,400
is neither due nor waiting for a
replacement.
